.TH GTKCRYPTO-CLI 1 "October 2026" "GTKCrypto 3.1.0"
.SH NAME
gtkcrypto-cli \- hash, encrypt, and decrypt files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gtkcrypto-cli hash
.RB [ -a
.IR NAME [, NAME ...]]
.RB [ -c " [" -q ]]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B gtkcrypto-cli encrypt
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B gtkcrypto-cli decrypt
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR FILE ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
New encrypted files use GTC3 with AES-256-GCM and Argon2id. Existing
destinations are not replaced unless
.B --force
is supplied. A
.I FILE
of
.B -
means standard input.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B hash
Compute one or more digests of each FILE in a single read pass, printed in
.BR sha256sum (1)
format. With
.B --check
the FILEs are checksum lists and every listed file is verified.
.TP
.B encrypt
Encrypt each FILE to FILE.enc, or to the path selected with
.BR --output .
.TP
.B decrypt
Decrypt v1, v2, or v3 GTKCrypto files. The output name drops a trailing
.B .enc
or, if there is none, gets
.B .decrypted
appended.
.PP
When several FILEs are given, a failure in one does not stop the others; the
exit status reports the first failure.
.SH HASH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -a ", " --algo " NAME[,NAME...]"
Algorithms to compute (default
.BR sha256 ).
Valid names are md5, sha1, sha256, sha384, sha512, sha3-256, sha3-384,
sha3-512, blake2b-256, blake2b-512, gost94 and whirlpool.
.TP
.BR -c ", " --check
Verify checksum lists (one algorithm only). Prints
.I name: OK
or
.I name: FAILED
for every entry and exits with status 1 if anything failed.
.TP
.BR -q ", " --quiet
With
.BR --check ,
do not print OK lines.
.SH ENCRYPT AND DECRYPT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -p ", " --password-file " PATH"
Read the password from the first line of PATH. The file must be a regular file
that is not accessible by group or others.
.TP
.BI --password-fd " N"
Read the password (first line) from file descriptor N.
.TP
.B --password-stdin
Read the password (first line) from standard input. Cannot be combined with
input from standard input.
.PP
Without any of these the password is read from the controlling terminal
(and confirmed when encrypting). New passwords need at least 8 characters;
existing data can be decrypted with any non-empty password.
.TP
.BR -o ", " --output " PATH"
Select the destination path, or
.B -
for standard output. Only valid with a single FILE.
.TP
.BR -f ", " --force
Atomically replace an existing destination after success.
.TP
.BR -d ", " --delete
Delete the source FILE after a successful operation.
.TP
.BI --kdf " LEVEL"
Encrypt only. Key derivation strength: \fBstandard\fR (default, 2 passes,
19 MiB), \fBstrong\fR (3 passes, 64 MiB) or \fBmaximum\fR (4 passes, 256 MiB).
The parameters are stored in the file, so decryption needs no option.
.TP
.BR -q ", " --quiet
Only print errors.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
Print one line per processed file and, on a terminal, a progress indicator.
.SH PIPES
Reading from standard input or writing to standard output is supported by
staging data in a private (mode 0600) temporary file in
.BR $TMPDIR ,
because the format records the plaintext length up front. The staging file is
removed afterwards, also when the operation is interrupted. Encrypted data is
never written to a terminal.
.PP
Example:
.EX
tar c dir | gtkcrypto-cli encrypt --password-file pw - > dir.tar.enc
gtkcrypto-cli decrypt --password-file pw - < dir.tar.enc | tar x
.EE
.SH SECURITY
GTKCrypto does not provide secure deletion. Protect password files with
appropriate filesystem permissions. Plaintext processed through pipes is
briefly stored in the temporary directory.
.PP
SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP during an operation cancel it cleanly and remove
temporary output files.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 indicates success, 1 an operational failure, 2 invalid command-line usage,
3 failed authentication, 4 an existing destination without
.BR --force ,
and 130 an interrupted operation.
